FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/voxel-flyover.1
    voxel-flyover [GODOT-OPTION...] [-- --autopilot SECONDS] [-- --day HOUR] [-- --tour SECOND] [-- --model FILE]
    Plays Voxel Flyover on the page: a corgi in a cape and sunglasses on a
    jet surfboard, a pelican on a bicycle pedalling under it for the jets,
    over a planet 600,000 units in radius, through its days and nights. The land runs on to a curved horizon, and far ranges rise
    over it as the board nears them. It flies itself until a click takes
    the board and gives the game the pointer: the mouse then steers, around
    and up and down, and the board flies the way it is headed at one speed.
    A ring flown through gives a boost, which wears off; rings stand in
    chains that climb, and the next ring of a chain is open for as long as
    the boost reaches it. The wheel zooms, T lets the hours run, Escape lets
    go of the board and the pointer, Q quits. On a touchscreen a touch
    takes the board: a finger's drag steers, two fingers zoom, and the ring
    at the top right gives the board back. A board that climbs past 420
    units is faster the higher it is: it leaves
    the clouds under it, the sky goes dark and the stars come out, and from
    some hundred thousand units up the planet is seen whole, with its day
    and night sides, its clouds, its seas' glint, its towns' lights and the
    rim of its air. Diving brings it back down, anywhere on the planet. A
    lap along the ground would take 35 hours. On a weak GPU it draws less,
    at an even rate. The number on the screen counts the rings passed. With
    --autopilot it flies by itself for that long and prints its frame rate,
    how even its frames were, the milliseconds a frame took the GPU and the
    renderer, the rings it passed, how high it went and what its frame
    shows; --day starts at that hour; --tour leaves the planet at that
    second, to the height --top gives (900000), and comes back. It draws
    through WebGPU (gpu@0). Its project is /usr/share/voxel-flyover/project:
    copy it to edit it with godot-editor. The flyer, board, jets, corgi and
    pelican, is /usr/share/voxel-flyover/flyer.glb, which Blender makes from
    the project's flyer.py (blender -b --python flyer.py, or Run Script in
    Blender's Text Editor); the game reads it as it starts, or the file
    --model names.
FILE /usr/share/voxel-flyover/voxel-flyover.pck
FILE /usr/share/voxel-flyover/flyer.glb
FOLDER /usr/share/voxel-flyover/project
